staging: an upload that fits the stream ring waits for the GPU instead of ending the session

At the last refusal of runs 40, 43, 44 and 45 the pool held no upload staging and nothing with the
GPU: 0 buffers, 0 finished, only pinned downloads. Asking again after a wait would find no new
memory. But the 128 MiB stream ring has been there since the start, and all a busy region needs is
time. So Request no longer throws for an upload of up to one region (8 MiB): when the ring is busy
and the pool refuses, it waits for the regions that upload needs and takes the ring. That covers
uniforms, query and small buffer and texture uploads with no change at their callers. Memory: none.

TryRequest does not wait. A decoded slice that finds nothing still goes next frame.

Only the regions' own tick is waited for. An older tick needs no submission. A tick still being
recorded does, and a submission in the middle of a draw's setup loses what is already recorded:
BindHostGeometryBuffers records the index and vertex binds, and the new command buffer starts
without them. InvalidateCommandBufferState sets the vertex flags again, but this draw has gone past
them, and nothing tracks the index buffer. ConfigureImpl now binds the geometry again when the tick
moved, just before ConfigureDraw. A guest read that flushes during UpdateRenderTargets had the same
gap. Descriptors are written after that point, and compute records nothing before its descriptors.

The wrap check moves above the first stamp, so a request that must wait stamps the ring with the
tick after the wait. That covers the regions not stamped yet, and no others.

A draw's uniforms can sit in a region already stamped with the old tick, or in a kept staging
buffer: a 4 KiB one can still be had when 8 MiB cannot. When a later request of the same setup
waits, the draw is recorded in the new tick while they read as free once the old tick is done, so
the ring can write over them and the pool can hand them out or destroy them. So when the wait
ended the command buffer, what the old tick covered gets the new one, in the ring and in the upload
cache. The regions this request needs keep their stamp: they are what was waited for, and with the
new tick the retry would find them busy and throw.

Two gaps stay. A flush from anything else in the setup restamps nothing, as before this patch. And
a setup that goes round the whole ring after its first uniform (128 MiB in one draw) writes over
that uniform: there is nothing left to wait for.

After a wait the ring's regions carry a tick still being recorded, so the next region may need a
submission of its own. Those are short, but a burst of refused uploads costs more of them.

Above 8 MiB Request can still throw. The next patch covers those callers.

